back here in New York and for the first  time ever two women are leading the  largest fire and police departments in  the country the FDNY and the NYPD Laura  Kavanaugh became the city's first female  fire commissioner just last month she  was acting commissioner since February  she's also one of the youngest in the  Department's history and earlier this  year commissioner kushant Sewell became  the first woman to lead the NYPD and  just the third black commissioner in  NYPD history only on CBS mornings they  join us for their first joint interview  a good morning Commissioners yeah good  morning it's a big deal both your  positions I want to get to the historic  nature of them but first since we just  had this coverage out of Colorado  Colorado Springs this tragedy at an  lgbtq nightclub commissioner Sewell I'd  love to know are there threats that  you're monitoring right now in the New  York City area anything that happens  across the country that horrific we  Monitor and we want to make sure that we  have our Assets in place to be able to  offer comfort for our communities we've  had some incidents in Hell's Kitchen  which are causing I'm concerned that we  have a number of resources devoted to as  well so we work well with our  communities we want to make sure that  they're safe and we certainly believe  that we have the resources to be able to  do that all right let's talk about the  work and the resources you to oversee  budgets in the billions tens of  thousands of people 30 000 officers uh  tens of thousands of of fire uh Fighters  as well as EMTs EMS inspectors what is  what does it mean starting with you uh  commissioner Kavanaugh what does it mean  to you to be in this role the first  woman to hold the position yeah I mean  it means a lot to me you know I hope to  inspire other people to see themselves  in positions that maybe they had never  thought of before I hope it opens the  door yeah what I think is fascinating  about you commissioner Kavanaugh I I  emcee to think for the fire department  and all the guys at the table were  telling me she's acting but she should  for sure have this job and what I  learned is that you went there on a  temporary basis commissioner Kavanaugh  you were only supposed to be there where  did you come from you're only supposed  to be there for a short period of time I  was in the mayor's office about eight  years ago so and we should also point  out just to clarify here I mean 98 of  the fire department is male right and  about 80 percent of the NYPD not the  leadership not anymore not anymore and  commissioner so how does it feel to to  thrive and be respected  um in such a male dominated industry I  keep saying that I think we need to  begin to normalize women in these roles  while these appointments are historic  and we don't take them lightly the work  we do is what matters and uh there we  cannot be the last in these roles um but  believe me I I think every single day  the men and women of the NYPD they are  truly extraordinary and the fact that  we've been able to uh get the support  that we need to do our job means a great  deal do you feel added pressure either  of you because you know people are  watching because of your gender because  of your race in your particular case do  you feel added pressure I would say not  pressure the spotlight yeah that's  definitely all the time it is bright and  it is hot at times yeah commissioner  Kavanaugh to get into a policy question  here scooters are all over these  electric scooters all over New York City  they're all over cities across the  country and there's been a bunch of  fires tied to them I think six people  have died according to your department  they've been more than 200 fires uh do  they need to be regulated in a new or  stronger way so we're working with the  mayor's office and the federal  government to actually look at exactly  that and the federal government actually  recently recalled a number of items that  we had pointed out so definitely we're  looking at better regulation and  commissioner Kavanaugh you you have not  been you were not a firefighter and some  people were thinking how can she run the  fire department if she hasn't been a  firefighter you don't see that as a  detriment though to what you do I don't  you know I'm I run big things my  benefits are most of my career and there  are a lot of people who run these  organizations who don't come up the  ranks you know they're huge  organizations I think of myself as an  advocate for our members and a CEO of a  large corporation commissioner so um  Tony mentioned headlines you know the  safety of people here in New York City  comes to mind especially in the subway  what are some of the things that you  might be heading to help people feel  safe in the city we all know the subway  is a lifeblood of the city we all depend  on it I took a Subway myself as a child  we recognized there were some challenges  in the subway so we surged thousands of  officers into the subway in cooperation  with our partners in the MTA we've  started making announcements on the  train to let people know that there are  police officers riding the trains and  their police officers on the platform  also when the train pulls into a station  where there is a Transit District you  will know that because there's going to  be an announcement that says that we  recognize that people feel safer when  they see an NYPD uniform the people just  completely exhale when they see a police  officer so having that omnipresence  having those people in the subway and  the officers engaging with the public is  important are there a lot of plain  clothes people on the subway there are  people you do not see in uniform okay  that's very good to know I've heard you  say uh commissioner Sewell that this is  a full circle moment for you because you  know they describe this as one of the  hardest jobs on the planet you call it  full circle but you can't imagine was it  something that you sought as a young  police officer did you seek this  position I thought the next thing  whatever the next thing was whatever the  next test was whatever the next  challenge was I was up for it and I  think both of us are about for this  challenge those lights may be hot but  we're ready for them oh and and  commissioner Kavanaugh you said you only  came there temporarily for the fire  department eight years later you were  still there why did you still stay what  is it that attracted what did you like  about it I mean it really is such an  amazing organization you know the men  and women who do this work are so  extraordinary you see it in some of  these recent events the roof rope rescue  a couple weeks ago I think you can  understand why if that's what you see  every day no matter how hard the day in  the office they inspire me they keep me  going and did you two know each other do  you hang out and say God can you believe  what is happening did you two know each  other we did not know each other before  this journey but we have become friends  since yeah all right I would love to be  a conversation I'd love to sit down at  lunch with the two of you I think that  could be really nice yeah that's a yes
 

